  "guide": {
   "buffPoints": [
    "**A buff is worth what it adds to its target.** +1 attack is worth 4 BP per hit it boosts, so on a mult 3 gun it is worth three times as much. Weapons are priced as if unbuffed, and the buff card pays for its leverage; mult weapons are the best targets for per-hit buffs.",
    "**+1 mult** is worth about 6 × the target's attack + 2 (5 to 31 at Mk I); **double damage** about the target's attack BP when it buffs two weapons, more for one; **an extra activation each round** about 1.5 × the target's attack BP.",
    "**Placement matters.** A buff that must sit in front of the armor, or that shields its target, dies early and is worth far less. If an effect costs more than the card can afford, give the card a weakness: charges, low health, or a condition.",
    "Measured at the card's best spot; a player won't always manage that, so prices sit a little under the measured mean."
   ],
   "findings": [
    "**The exterior formula (2026-09-30).** A weapon's worth, measured by swapping one slot in mirrored boards: behind the armor about 4 × attack + 1 × health; in front of it about 4 × attack + 1.9 × health − 7. Health's worth depends on how early the card gets hit: a 24-health wall is worth about 41 in front, 24 right behind the armor, and 10 at the back. Each card is priced for the best place it may stand, so moving the armor around gains nothing.",
    "Where cards go: sturdy weapons right behind the armor (a 4/16 is worth 38 there, 29 at the back), glass cannons at the back (a 5/4 is worth 22 right behind the armor, 25.5 at the back).",
    "Buffs favor mult: with +1 attack on every weapon, 1-attack ×3 shapes rise from about 43% to 62% and big single shots fall to about 43%. Weapons are priced unbuffed; buff cards pay for their leverage (Buff prices).",
    "With Shields {shields}, Armor {armor}, hull {hull} and a 25% support bonus (a stand-in for the interior, since removed), fights ran about 8 rounds with four weapons a side and 7 with six: on target. With no support they run about 9.",
    "Pricing attack cheaper shortens fights when card shapes stay fixed, because the same budget buys more attack and more health. At {atkExt} BP, fights land on target and balanced, attack-heavy and multi-hit shapes come out even. Cheaper, and attack-heavy shapes pull ahead.",
    "Put every weapon behind the Armor. A gun in front of the Armor dies before the Armor does and loses its damage early: one Cannon among three Lasers won 80% behind the Armor and 44% in front of it.",
    "Health-heavy weapons (1 attack to 3 health) lose at every price. The front line already supplies the time, so a weapon's damage matters far more than its health. At Mk I, keep a plain weapon's attack at 4 or more. A tanky weapon needs a defensive effect to earn its slot.",
    "Under the old row phases the ship that acts first wins 85% to 90% of even fights. Alternating card by card, faster ship first, cuts that to about 55%.",
    "Weapons with 1 or 2 attack lose to nearly everything at every exchange rate tested: every ship's large front line makes health cheap and damage precious.",
    "A ship whose exterior row is gone has no guns, so hull only buys time. It matters against boarders, Pierce, hull hitters and the Maelstrom.",
    "**The interior (2026-09-30).** Prices assume the reference enemy (DESIGN 6.8.2): an interior of two support cards and one defender, and boarders in half of all fights (30% one, 20% two).",
    "**Boarding is about numbers, not stats.** The defender acts first on its own ship, so equal crews stall: one boarder against one defender is worth about 0 at every crew size tested. Two or three boarding cards against the reference interior are worth 9–15; against an empty interior, a 4-attack boarder or two boarders are worth about 60. Boarders that beat the defense go on to the hull.",
    "**Interior defense matters only against two or more boarders:** a defender is worth 2–7 against one boarder and 10–58 against two. Walls and defenders are about equal.",
    "**Always-on buffs end with their card** (DESIGN 7.3.4), so boarders that kill support cards take their buffs away; one slow boarder rarely gets there before the buffed weapon dies anyway.",
    "**Measure with reference weapons, not plate health.** A plain plate's health behind the armor is worth less than 1 BP a point and breaks against aim effects and boarders, so prices measured in plate health run about 25% high (the exterior buff table was measured that way). Raw win rates on mirrored boards saturate: one extra 12 BP plate wins 99%."
   ],
